/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Setting out, also called layout surveying, places a designed point on the ground from known control.
   - Horizontal control fixes easting and northing; vertical control fixes reduced level.
   - The coordinate method stores control and target coordinates in the total station.
   - ΔE and ΔN are easting and northing differences in metres; α is azimuth clockwise from north, with atan2 taking easting first; D is horizontal distance; β is the setting-out angle from the backsight, reduced to 0° to 360°.
   - Field steps: set on the control station, sight the backsight, set the horizontal circle to 0°00′00″ on it, turn the angle β clockwise, measure D, and drive the peg.
   - Construction surveys repeat this at each stage: site clearing, foundation corners, columns, road centreline, drainage gradients, and deformation monitoring.
   - Levels on the pegs.
*/
