/* book Worked example: Q: Control station A is (E 1000.000 m, N 1000.000 m) with backsight azimuth 0°00′00″. A building corner C is to be set at (E 1025.000 m, N 1040.000 m). Find the setting-out angle from the backsight and the horizontal distance AC.
   1. ΔE = 1025.000 − 1000.000 = +25.000 m.
   2. ΔN = 1040.000 − 1000.000 = +40.000 m.
   3. Both positive: C is north-east of A, so α lies between 0° and 90°.
   4. D = √(25.000² + 40.000²) = √(625 + 1600) = √2225 = 47.1699 m ≈ 47.170 m.
   5. α = atan2(25.000, 40.000) = 32.00538° = 32°00′19″.
   6. β = α − backsight azimuth = 32°00′19″ − 0°00′00″ = 32°00′19″ clockwise.
   Answer: Turn 32°00′19″ clockwise from the backsight and measure 47.170 m horizontally from A to peg C. */
