/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An echo sounder has a transducer under the boat.
   - d is depth below the transducer in metres, c is sound speed in the water in m/s, and t is the two-way travel time in seconds.
   - This is the same travel-time idea as liDAR and laser scanning, with sound in water instead of light in air.
   - Bathymetry is water depth measurement.
   - Chart datum is a low-water reference, close to lowest astronomical tide.
   - A bathymetric chart plots reduced depth at position, with shoreline and symbols.
   - Fixing the boat.
   - Underground survey begins at surface control.
   - An underground traverse starts at the shaft station.
   - Levels transfer by tape or laser; check against a surface benchmark.
*/
