/* book Worked example: Q: An echo sounder whose transducer is 0.50 m below the water surface records a two-way travel time of 0.040 s. Sound speed in the water is 1500 m/s. The tide is 1.20 m above chart datum. Find the depth below the water surface and the reduced depth below chart datum.
   1. One-way time = t/2 = 0.040/2 = 0.020 s.
   2. Depth below the transducer d = c × one-way time = 1500 × 0.020 = 30.00 m.
   3. Depth below the water surface = d + draft = 30.00 + 0.50 = 30.50 m.
   4. The surface stands 1.20 m above chart datum, so the bed is 30.50 − 1.20 = 29.30 m below datum.
   5. Check: at a low water exactly on chart datum (h = 0) the sounder would read 29.30 m below the surface at this spot.
   Answer: Depth below the water surface 30.50 m; reduced depth below chart datum 29.30 m. */
